Given the function $f(x)= \frac{4^{x}+1}{2^{ax}}(a∈R)$ is an even function, $g(x)=t⋅2^{x}+4$,
(1) Find the value of $a$;
(2) When $t=-2$, find the solution set of $f(x) < g(x)$;
(3) If the graph of function $f(x)$ is always above the graph of $g(x)$, find the range of real number $t$.

s:
(1) Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we have $f(x)=f(-x)$, that is, $\frac{4^{x}+1}{2^{ax}}= \frac{4^{-x}+1}{2^{-ax}}$,
Simplifying, we get $2^{2ax}=4^{x}$, hence $\boxed{a=1}$;

(2) $f(x) < g(x)$ implies $\frac{4^{x}+1}{2^{x}} < -2\cdot 2^{x}+4$, which simplifies to $3\cdot4^{x}-4\cdot2^{x}+1 < 0$,
Thus, $\frac{1}{3} < 2^{x} < 1$, or $\log_{2} \frac{1}{3} < x < 0$,
So the solution set of the inequality $f(x) < g(x)$ is $\boxed{\{x|\log_{2} \frac{1}{3} < x < 0\}}$;

(3) As the graph of function $f(x)$ is always above the graph of $g(x)$,
we have $f(x) > g(x)$, that is, $\frac{4^{x}+1}{2^{x}} > t\cdot 2^{x}+4$, which gives $t < \frac{1}{4^{x}}- \frac{4}{2^{x}}+1$,
Since $\frac{1}{4^{x}}- \frac{4}{2^{x}}+1=(\frac{1}{2^{x}}-2)^{2}-3\geqslant -3$, we have $\boxed{t < -3}$;

Therefore, the range of real number $t$ is $t < -3$.

