Given $\frac{x}{i}=1-i$, we aim to find the value of $x$.

First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $i$ to isolate $x$:
\begin{align*}
x &= (1-i) \cdot i \\
&= i - i^2.
\end{align*}

Recalling that $i^2 = -1$, we substitute this into our equation:
\begin{align*}
x &= i - (-1) \\
&= 1 + i.
\end{align*}

Therefore, we conclude that $x = \boxed{1+i}$.

